  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThis afternoon sunshine was abundant across the U.P. with visible \nsatellite imagery showing clear skies over a large expanse of the \nnorthern U.S. including the Great Lakes region. Water vapor imagery \nand model height analysis continued to show mainly zonal flow over \nthe northern tier of the U.S. along with an upper low that had \ncut off from the flow digging down the Pacific Coast of the U.S.\nWith abundant dry air and clear skies it was a chilly start to \nthe day across the U.P. with temperatures in the 20s and 30s. \nHowever, temperatures had recovered nicely with most locations \nin the lower 60s early this afternoon under light southerly \nwinds.\n\nThrough the remainder of the week the upper low pressure off the \nCalifornia coast will lift northeast through the intermountain west \nand into Saskatchewan, phasing with the upper level jet. This will \namplify the flow downstream with ridging strengthening over the Upper \nGreat Lakes. This will help continue our streak of warm afternoon\nhighs some 10-20F above normal. With the dry air in place \nnights will continue to be chilly, dropping into the 30s until \nlate in the week and weekend, as strengthening southerly winds \nhelp to keep temperatures in the 40s overnight.\n\nA surface low pressure tracking well north of the area will keep \nsoutherly winds in place through tonight. This, combined with strong\nradiational cooling and winds off of Lake Michigan will bring patchy\nfog to the south-central and eastern U.P. tonight. Winds slowly turn \nback to the north and east on Wednesday afternoon and evening as \nanother high pressure settles over the area. Low level moisture and \na strengthening subsidence inversion could result in widespread fog \nand low clouds developing late Wednesday night and lingering into \nmuch of the morning hours, before lifting in the afternoon with \ndiurnal heating and returning southerly flow.\n\nAs low pressure tracks north of the area this weekend southerly flow \nwill strengthen resulting in breezy conditions Saturday, followed by \nbreezy westerly winds behind the cold front Sunday. While the \nair isn't much colder behind the initial front it will be \nnotably drier which could result in borderline elevated fire \nweather conditions on Sunday. While showers will accompany the \nfront they are expected to remain rather hit-and-miss and \nappreciable rainfall is not expected.\n\nEarly next week ensembles are trending towards a period of cooler \nweather that could lead to at least some lake effect precipitation \nand high temperatures closer to or below seasonal averages. The\nNBM has a 40-75% chance for much of the U.P. to remain below \n50F on Monday and Tuesday.",
